## Context

Before this module existed, the server had no single owner of "which brain
backend is wired and how does an agent reach into it." The closest thing was
the existing detection helper that did one job — read the host's MCP config,
find the `obsidian` key, stat the configured vault path, and return a
structured state. Everything past detection was scattered: the `/aide:brain`
command prose named `mcp__obsidian__*` tools by hand, agent definitions
declared `mcpServers: [obsidian]` in their frontmatter, and skill descriptions
referenced obsidian-side concepts as if obsidian were the only possible
backend. Adding a second backend (Notion, GitHub Wiki, hosted PKB, anything
else) would have meant editing every one of those surfaces in lockstep — a
fan-out the surface was never built to carry, and a guarantee that some
surface would lag behind and tell agents to call tools that don't exist for
the wired backend.

The settled architectural insight that shapes this module is two-edged.
First, backend identity is no longer carried by the MCP key. Upstream of
this registry, the install service writes every brain-class entry under the
fixed key `brain` regardless of which backend is actually wired underneath
it; that collapses three coupling problems at once (uniform agent
frontmatter, uniform `mcp__brain__*` tool naming in instruction prose,
single-key detection) but it pushes one new problem onto this module —
the key alone tells the registry nothing, so identity has to be inferred
from what the entry actually launches. Second, the brain owns its own
navigation rules after the entry-point file. The install service seeds a
`CLAUDE.md` at the brain root that names folder structure, search
semantics, decision protocol, and write rules; once an agent reads that
file, the brain itself takes over and the server has no further role in
navigation. So the only piece of backend knowledge that has to live here
is the prose that gets the agent to the seeded entry-point — never the
operations past it.

The boundary that makes this clean is "until the agent is reading the
seeded entry-point file, the registry owns the navigation prose; from
that file forward, the brain owns it." A naive implementation would
encode read/search/write per backend in the registry to "save" the agent
a hop into the seeded file — and would replicate every navigation rule
the seeded file already owns, drift the moment the user customized their
vault, and turn every backend addition into a per-backend translation
layer with its own CRUD surface. The opposite naive implementation would
match on the MCP key and break on day one of the fixed-key world the
upstream invariant just installed.

This module lives at the service layer, peer to the install service, not
as a helper of any single tool. Two distinct consumers depend on it: the
detector that resolves a backend driver during runtime brain-state
construction, and the prose composer that renders ready-to-execute
instructions for the runtime brain tool. Neither owns the registry —
they both read from it. Putting the registry under either consumer
would force the other to reach across a sibling boundary; making it a
peer service is what lets both call in cleanly and what lets future
consumers (think: a doc-generator that lists supported backends, an
upgrade tool that audits wired entries) call in without the existing
consumers learning anything new.

## Strategy

**Matchers pattern-match on the entry's command/args shape, not on the
MCP key.** The fixed `brain` key (settled in `.aide/handoff.aide`) means
the key alone tells the registry nothing about which backend is wired —
every brain-class entry is keyed the same way. Identity has to come from
what the entry actually launches. The Obsidian matcher inspects the
entry's `command` and `args` for the launcher signature the install
service writes (a positional arg referencing `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`, with
the vault path as the final positional arg). This is the load-bearing
choice that makes "adding a new backend = one matcher entry" true: the
key cannot disambiguate, so the entry shape must. Keying off `brain`
would silently mis-identify every future backend the moment a second
matcher arrived.

**The registry exposes a dual-accessor surface — one resolver from
entry, one lookup by id — backed by a single matcher table.** The
detector resolves a driver from a wired entry once during brain-state
construction; downstream consumers (the prose composer, programmatic
auditors) only have the resolved id and should not have to re-derive the
entry shape just to reach the registry again. Two accessors, one source
of truth. Why one table not two: the spec's `outcomes.desired` rule
"every driver returned by the resolver is retrievable by id" cashes out
structurally, not through tests-on-tests — the accessors are two views
over the same data, so they cannot drift. This closes the
`outcomes.undesired` line forbidding inconsistency between accessors.

**A driver carries exactly two fields: a stable identifier string and
a function that renders the entry-point reach prose.** Two fields, no
more. The spec's load-bearing constraint is "registry only carries
entry-point reach"; adding fields like an entry-point path or a
backend-side MCP tool name would tempt consumers to dispatch on those
fields directly (`if entry-point path matches X, do Y`) instead of
asking the driver to render the prose, which would re-decentralize the
backend knowledge the registry was built to centralize. Everything
per-backend lives behind the prose-rendering function — the consumer
calls it, gets prose, and is done.

**The prose-rendering function takes the resolved vault path and
nothing else.** The vault path (or whatever post-detection state the
backend's prose needs to reference) is the only piece of state the
current backend's prose has to know — the matcher already extracted it,
and the seeded entry-point file lives at a deterministic location
inside it. If a future backend's prose needs more — an API token, a
workspace id, a hosted endpoint — the input shape grows by one field,
but only when proven necessary by a real backend's needs. Speculatively
widening the parameter shape now would force the existing matcher to
populate fields it doesn't need and would muddy the contract. This
follows the parent project's decision-form rule: ship the smallest
shape that the current backends prove necessary, grow when the next
backend forces it.

**Failure prose is not the registry's responsibility.** When detection
returns "no entry wired" or "vault path doesn't resolve on disk," there
is no driver to render those messages — by definition, the registry's
matchers haven't fired or the matched driver is for a backend whose
vault is unreachable. The remediation prose for those branches is the
same regardless of which backend would have been wired (run
`/aide:brain config`), so it lives in the prose composer that wraps the
registry, not behind a per-backend template. The registry's own
contract is "given an entry, return a driver or null"; null is a value,
not an exception. This closes the `outcomes.undesired` line that
forbids throwing on unrecognized entries — the consumer collapses null
into the `no-mcp-entry` branch and composes its own remediation.

**The driver and entry types are defined in the project's central
types module and re-exported through this service's public face.** The
detector, the prose composer, and the runtime brain tool's response
type all reference these shared definitions; centralizing the type
declarations means consumers do not reach into this module's internals
to grab a type, and the type's evolution is visible at the project's
shared boundary. This honors the `outcomes.desired` rule pinning shared
type definitions at the central module.

**Matchers and instruction prose are co-located inside this module, not
factored across separate "matchers" and "templates" subfolders.** Today
there is one matcher and one prose template — splitting them by
function instead of by backend would create three folders per backend
and an empty matcher-table file with one entry. The forcing function
that should drive a future split is per-backend complexity, not type of
content: when a single backend's matcher predicate or prose template
grows past roughly fifty lines, factor that backend into a sibling
subfolder under this module — one folder per backend, matcher and prose
together. Until then, inline.

**The registry returns null for unrecognized wirings — never throws,
never falls back to a default backend.** When the entry under the fixed
`brain` key matches no registered matcher (the user wired something the
server doesn't recognize), the resolver returns null and the consumer
collapses that into the same remediation as "not wired at all" —
re-run `/aide:brain config` to wire to a recognized backend. Falling
back to a default backend would silently dispatch the wrong prose;
throwing would surface as an MCP protocol error with no structured
remediation. The consumer's contract requires structured-state
handling at every layer, so the registry surfaces "no driver" as a
value. This closes the `outcomes.undesired` lines forbidding throws and
forbidding silent fallback.

**Matcher predicates are pure synchronous functions over the entry
shape.** No I/O, no async, no side effects. The registry is a pure
dispatch table — its job is to inspect a small object and return
true/false. Keeping the predicates synchronous and side-effect-free
means the registry can be called freely during detection's hot path
without dragging an extra await; it also means the matcher table is
inspectable for testing without standing up fake filesystems. The one
async hop in brain-state construction is the detector reading the host
MCP config; the registry runs entirely on the data the detector
already has in hand.

**The Obsidian matcher and prose template are this module's only
backend today, but the contract is roster-shaped only structurally —
not by count.** The `outcomes.undesired` line that forbids hardcoding
a specific backend count or treating Obsidian as a special case in
dispatch logic is the load-bearing constraint here: tomorrow's Notion
matcher (or hosted-PKB matcher) lands as a single new entry in the
matcher table and a single new prose template, no consumer touches.
"One-to-one between matcher and driver" and "accessor consistency" are
the contract; "Obsidian is the one we ship today" is an implementation
fact that the consumer surface cannot see.

## Good examples

**A wired Obsidian entry resolves to its driver, which renders the
entry-point reach prose.** The host's `.mcp.json` has the brain key
wired with the standard Obsidian launcher shape:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "brain": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "/Users/dev/my-brain"]
    }
  }
}
```

The detector reads that entry and hands it to the resolver. The
resolver inspects `command` and `args`, sees the `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`
signature in the positional args, and returns the Obsidian driver —
identifier `obsidian`, with a prose-rendering function. The detector
records `backend: "obsidian"` on the resolved brain-state and
forwards it to the prose composer.

The composer asks the driver to render its prose with the resolved
vault path:

> Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to read CLAUDE.md at the vault root
> (`/Users/dev/my-brain/CLAUDE.md`). That file is the brain's
> navigation contract — folder structure, search protocol, decision
> protocol, and where-to-find-things. Once you have read it, follow
> the rules it defines for the rest of your task; the brain takes
> over from there and this server has no further role in your
> navigation.

The prose names exactly one MCP tool (`mcp__brain__read_note`), one
file (`CLAUDE.md`), and one location (the vault root). It tells the
agent in one sentence what's in the file (without enumerating any of
its content) and ends with the "brain takes over" handoff. The agent
acts on it directly: it calls the named read tool on the named path,
reads the seeded navigation contract, and proceeds.

**A wired entry that doesn't match any registered matcher resolves
to null.** The host's `.mcp.json` has the brain key wired to some
other launcher the registry doesn't know:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "brain": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["@some-org/notion-pkb-server", "workspace-abc-123"]
    }
  }
}
```

The detector hands the entry to the resolver. The Obsidian matcher's
predicate inspects the args, sees no `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` signature,
returns false. No other matcher is registered today, so the resolver
returns null. The detector records `backend: null` on the resolved
brain-state and the status falls through to `no-mcp-entry` — the
consumer's prose composer renders the standard remediation pointing
the user at `/aide:brain config` to re-wire to a recognized backend.
No throw, no default-backend fallback, no obsidian prose on a
non-obsidian wiring.

**Driver lookup by identifier returns the same driver the resolver
returned.** A consumer that already knows the resolved identifier
(say, the prose composer running off a previously-built brain-state
where `backend === "obsidian"` is already populated) asks the
registry for the Obsidian driver by id. The lookup returns the same
driver shape the resolver would have returned for an Obsidian-shaped
entry — same identifier, same prose-rendering function. A lookup for
an unknown id (`notion`, given today's roster) returns null. The two
accessors agree because they read from the same matcher table.

## Bad examples

**A matcher that pattern-matches on the MCP key.** The matcher
predicate inspects `entry.key === "obsidian"` (or `=== "brain"`)
instead of the entry's `command` and `args`.

```
predicate: entry => entry.key === "obsidian"
```

Violates the `outcomes.undesired` line that forbids keying off the MCP
key. The fixed-key invariant means every brain-class entry is keyed
`brain` upstream, so this predicate either matches every brain-class
wiring (the `entry.key === "brain"` variant) or never matches anything
at all (the `entry.key === "obsidian"` variant). In the first variant
it silently mis-identifies every future Notion or hosted-PKB wiring as
Obsidian; in the second it silently fails to identify any wiring. The
registry must inspect what the entry launches, never the key it sits
under.

**A driver whose prose-rendering function returns an empty string.**

```
renderInstructions: () => ""
```

Violates the `outcomes.undesired` line that forbids empty or bare-
reference prose. The composer takes the empty string and forwards it
as the runtime brain tool's `instructions` field — the agent sees
`status: "ok"` with no usable prose, assumes the brain is reachable,
and either fails later when it tries to read something or skips the
brain entirely without telling the user. Prose must always be
non-empty, multi-sentence, and ready-to-execute.

**A driver whose prose enumerates folder structure, search rules, or
write rules.** The Obsidian driver's prose says:

> Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to read CLAUDE.md at the vault root.
> Then follow the wikilink crawling protocol: hub notes are
> navigation, content notes are depth 0, never re-read, stay in
> scope. Coding conventions go through the study-playbook skill;
> everything else uses `mcp__brain__search_notes` scoped to the
> right directory. Research lives under `research/`, retros under
> `process/retro/`, the playbook under `coding-playbook/`...

Violates the `outcomes.undesired` line that forbids duplicating what
the seeded entry-point file owns. Folder structure, the wikilink
crawling protocol, the decision protocol, and the where-to-find-things
table are all written into the seeded `CLAUDE.md` at provision time.
Re-stating them in the registry's prose creates two sources of truth
that drift the moment the user customizes their vault, and bloats
every brain reach with information the agent is one read away from
anyway. The registry's prose stops at the entry-point — it names the
read tool, names the file, says "the brain takes over," and stops.

**A consumer branching on the resolved identifier directly.** The
runtime brain tool's response composer contains:

```
if (driver.id === "obsidian") {
  return obsidianSpecificProse(state.vaultPath);
}
```

Violates the `outcomes.undesired` line that forbids branching on the
backend identifier outside this module. Per-backend logic hardcoded
in a consumer is a registry the registry doesn't own — every such
branch is a place a future backend addition will be missed, exactly
the fan-out the registry was built to centralize. The composer must
dispatch through the driver (`driver.renderInstructions(state)`) so
adding a Notion driver tomorrow lights up the runtime brain tool with
zero consumer changes.

**A driver shape that includes a CRUD-style API.** A new driver shape
emerges with `read(path)`, `search(query)`, and `write(path, content)`
methods alongside the prose-rendering function:

```
{
  id: "obsidian",
  read: (path) => mcp.call("mcp__brain__read_note", { path }),
  search: (query) => mcp.call("mcp__brain__search_notes", { query }),
  write: (path, content) => mcp.call("mcp__brain__write_note", { path, content }),
  renderInstructions: state => "..."
}
```

Violates the `outcomes.undesired` line that forbids encoding brain
operations in the driver and the line that forbids a CRUD-style proxy
layer. The registry is data plus prose, not a translation layer; the
seeded entry-point file already orchestrates the same operations,
and the agent calls the backend's native MCP tools directly after
reading the prose. Every CRUD method on the driver is a per-backend
wrapper that has to be maintained alongside the seeded file's rules,
and the two drift the moment they disagree.

**Two accessors that drift.** The resolver-by-entry returns a driver
with identifier `obsidian` and a prose-rendering function. The
lookup-by-id for `obsidian` returns a different driver with a stale
prose-rendering function from a prior version, or returns null even
though the resolver registered the driver. Either way:

```
resolveBackend(obsidianEntry).renderInstructions !== getDriverById("obsidian").renderInstructions
```

Violates the `outcomes.undesired` line that forbids inconsistency
between accessors. The two accessors must derive from a single
matcher table so they cannot disagree — registering a driver in two
places, or maintaining two parallel data structures the resolver and
lookup read from independently, is the structural anti-pattern this
rule exists to prevent.

**A matcher that throws on an unrecognized wiring.** The resolver
walks its matcher table, no predicate fires, and the resolver throws:

```
throw new Error(`No backend driver registered for entry: ${JSON.stringify(entry)}`)
```

Violates the `outcomes.undesired` line that forbids throwing.
"Unrecognized wiring" is a normal user state (they wired something
the server doesn't recognize yet, or they typo'd a launcher), not an
exceptional one. The consumer's contract is structured-state at every
layer; a throw surfaces as an MCP protocol error with no
`instructions` field for the agent to act on, defeating the spec's
"never silent, never throw" invariant. The resolver must return null
and let the consumer compose remediation prose.

## References

- `.aide/handoff.aide` -- settled architectural decisions: brain MCP
  key fixed at `brain`, backend identity inferred from command/args
  shape, brain owns navigation past the entry-point, `instructions`
  is pre-formatted prose not structured fields. Anchors every
  Strategy decision in this spec.
- `src/service/brainBackends/.aide` (frontmatter) -- the intent
  paragraph and outcomes drove every paragraph: dual-accessor
  consistency, two-field driver shape, null-not-throw on unrecognized
  wirings, matchers-on-shape-not-key, prose-stops-at-entry-point.
- `src/tools/brain/.aide` and `src/tools/brain/plan.aide` -- the
  consumer that drove this prerequisite into existence; named the
  resolver-by-entry contract this module exposes, the
  `BackendDriver` and `McpServerEntry` type shapes the registry's
  central-types declaration carries, and the dual-accessor
  resolution that this module's matcher table backs.
- `src/service/install/.aide` -- closest sibling service spec;
  reference for the decision-form Strategy shape and the "service
  layer is consumer-agnostic, peer to other services not child of
  any tool" placement convention this module follows.
- `src/tools/info/buildBrainState/index.ts` -- existing detection
  logic that the lifted `buildBrainState` consumer will compose
  with this registry; confirmed the entry-shape extraction pattern
  (last positional arg as vault path) the Obsidian matcher
  inherits, and confirmed the structured-state-not-throw contract
  the registry's null-return mirrors.
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts` -- defines the
  Obsidian launcher shape the matcher predicates against
  (`@bitbonsai/mcpvault` package, vault path as final positional
  arg, platform-conditional `cmd /c` wrapper on Windows) and
  confirms the seeded entry-point file is `CLAUDE.md` at the vault
  root — the path the Obsidian driver's prose names.
- `src/.aide` -- parent submodule spec; established the
  no-doctrine-as-string-literals invariant (the seeded `CLAUDE.md`
  is the single source of truth for navigation rules, never
  duplicated in a registry's prose) that shapes the
  prose-stops-at-entry-point decision.
